      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Machines</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. They were chosen from published specifications,
            maker product pages, shipping and warranty terms, recall records and independent
            coverage, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from resistance type rather than brand, because it decides most of what
            owning an all-in-one gym is like: a selectorized weight stack, flexible rods, and an
            incline glideboard that uses your own body weight. For each we looked for a model the
            maker listed as on sale on 19 October 2026, preferably one also sold on Amazon US, and
            that an independent source described in enough detail to cross-check. The independent
            sources were BarBend's reviews of the Marcy MWM-990 and BowFlex Xtreme 2 SE, Active.com's
            BowFlex coverage, <a href="/academy-sports-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Academy Sports</a>' listing for the Marcy, Iron Company's listing for the
            Best Fitness gym, Amazon's listings for the Body-Solid and Total Gym machines, and a
            second retailer's listing for the EXM2500S.
          </p>
          <p>
            Disagreements were common, and they are the reason several cards carry fewer numbers
            than you might expect. The Marcy MWM-990 is 42 inches wide on Marcy's page and 36 on
            BarBend's; the BowFlex Xtreme 2 SE is 63 inches long on BowFlex's page and 53 inches in
            two reviews; the Body-Solid EXM2500S weighs 578 lb on its product page and 446 lb on its
            spec sheet; and the Best Fitness BFMG20 is sold with a 145 lb stack by Body-Solid and
            listed at 150 lb by several retailers. Total Gym's own page for its FIT Anniversary
            Edition gives both a 450 lb and a 400 lb user limit, and both a two-year and a six-month
            parts warranty, which is one reason that model is not carded here.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page was checked against the CPSC recall database by recall title,
            manufacturer and recall description. BowFlex (formerly Nautilus, now owned by Johnson
            Health Tech) has home gym recalls on record for the Power Pro, Ultimate and Ultimate 2 —
            none of them current models — along with its well-known June 2025 SelectTech 552 and
            1090 dumbbell recall, covered in our
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbell guide</a>.
            Total Gym has two recalls from 2002 for older models. Marcy's importer, Impex, recalled
            a set of Olympic dumbbell handles in 2021, not a home gym. We found no recalls for
            Body-Solid or Best Fitness, or for the marketplace brands discussed below. The used-gym
            section lists the old recalls in detail, because those machines still change hands.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also left several machines out on purpose. BowFlex's PR3000 is still on its website
            but could not be ordered on 19 October. BowFlex's Revolution, which uses SpiraFlex
            resistance plates, is on sale at $2,299 after a discount, but we could not find an
            independent source to confirm its figures that day. Marketplace stack gyms sold under
            names such as SincMill and Hulfuly are widely listed on Amazon, but we could not find a
            specification published anywhere other than the listings themselves, and listings for
            what appears to be the same frame give different stack weights; we explain how to judge
            them in the section on reading a listing. Functional trainers are covered on our cable
            machine page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resistance-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Stack vs Rod vs Band vs Bodyweight: How Each Machine Makes Resistance</h2>
<p>
            Every all-in-one home gym is built around one way of making resistance, and it shapes
            the feel of every exercise, the weight of the machine, and how far it can grow with you.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight stack (Marcy, Body-Solid)</h3>
          <p>
            A pile of steel or cast-iron plates on two guide rods, lifted by a cable. You choose the
            load with a pin, and the plates rise and fall through every rep. A stack feels the same at
            the start of a movement as at the end, because gravity does not change halfway through,
            and it is the design closest to the machines in a commercial gym. The costs are weight and
            size: the stack alone is 145 to 210 lb on this page, the assembled machines are heavier
            still, and adding load usually means buying the maker's add-on plates. Choose a stack if
            you want gym-style feel and expect to train on the machine for years.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Flexible rods (BowFlex Xtreme 2 SE)</h3>
          <p>
            BowFlex's Power Rods are nylon composite rods that bend as you pull a cable hooked to
            them, rated in pounds and clipped on in combinations. Rods weigh a fraction of a stack, so
            the machine is light enough to move and to put upstairs, and there is very little inertia,
            which BowFlex describes as joint-friendly. The trade-off is the resistance curve: like a
            bow, a rod resists more the further it bends, so the effort rises through each rep and is
            lightest at the start. Choose rods if machine weight, a smooth start and a lower price
            matter more than gym-machine feel.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Spring-steel bands and plates (BowFlex Revolution and similar)</h3>
          <p>
            A few machines, including BowFlex's Revolution, use coiled or stacked spring-steel
            resistance units rather than iron or rods. BowFlex says its SpiraFlex plates give a
            linear feel closer to a weight stack than rods do. Band-type systems share the rod
            advantages of low machine weight and low inertia, and generally cost more than a rod gym.
            We have not carded one this year, for the reason in the section above. Elastic resistance
            bands, the cheapest version of the idea, are a separate purchase covered in our
            <a href="/best-resistance-bands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">resistance band guide</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Incline bodyweight (Total Gym)</h3>
          <p>
            You lie, sit or kneel on a glideboard that rolls up a pair of rails, and pull or push
            yourself up the incline using cables or the squat stand. The resistance is a share of your
            own body weight, set by the angle of the rails. There is no iron, the frame folds nearly
            flat, and most models arrive assembled. The limit is load: the heaviest setting is still a
            fraction of your body weight, which is plenty for many people and not enough for strong
            lifters on lower-body work. Choose this for low ceilings, shared rooms and gentle
            progression.
          </p>
          <p>
            A rough guide to choosing: if you want to lift progressively heavier for years, buy a
            stack. If the gym has to go upstairs, be moved, or cost under a thousand dollars with
            more than 150 lb of headline resistance, rods are the practical choice. If ceiling
            height or storage decides everything, buy an incline trainer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="real-resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Real Resistance vs Advertised Resistance</h2>
<p>
            The headline number on an all-in-one gym is the easiest figure to misread. Here is what
            each kind of number really tells you.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A stack figure is the pin, not every station</h3>
          <p>
            A 150 lb stack means 150 lb of plates. What you feel at each station depends on how the
            cable is routed and on the lever arms of the press and leg attachments. Some stations
            pull close to the pinned weight, some less, and some more: Body-Solid says its optional
            <a href="/best-leg-press-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>leg press</a> for the EXM2500S works at a 2:1 ratio, so the 210 lb stack becomes 420 lb of
            resistance at the footplate. Few makers of budget stack gyms publish a per-station
            figure, so assume the chest press and leg developer will not feel the same as the
            number on the pin, and judge progress against your own previous sessions rather than
            against a gym machine. Our
            <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/#pulley-ratio" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">explanation of 2:1 and 1:1 pulley ratios</a>
            covers the cable side of this in more detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A rod figure is a rating at full bend</h3>
          <p>
            BowFlex rates the Xtreme 2 SE at 210 lb, made up of one pair of 5 lb rods, two pairs of
            10 lb rods, one pair of 30 lb rods and one pair of 50 lb rods, and says that gives 5 to
            105 lb per side in 5 lb steps. Two things are worth knowing. First, the 210 lb total is
            both sides together; a one-arm movement can use at most the 105 lb on its side. Second,
            BowFlex's own footnote says the rod ratings are estimates, that resistance can vary with
            environment and use, and that rod resistance normally degrades over time and with
            extensive use. Because a rod resists less at the start of a rep than at the end, a 50 lb
            rod does not feel like a 50 lb plate through the whole movement. That is a different
            feel, not a defect, but it means the upgrade to 310 or 410 lb buys more than it sounds
            like at the end of the rep and less at the start.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A bodyweight figure is a share of you</h3>
          <p>
            On an incline trainer the load is the part of your body weight acting along the rails,
            which grows with the angle. As simple arithmetic, ignoring friction and the weight of the
            glideboard: at a 10-degree rail angle the pull along the rails is about 17 percent of
            body weight, at 30 degrees about half, and at 45 degrees about 70 percent. For a
            180 lb person that is roughly 30, 90 and 127 lb. Total Gym describes the EVOLVE's
            resistance as 16 levels rather than in pounds, and its real angles may differ from these
            round numbers, so treat them as an illustration of why the top levels feel much harder
            than the bottom ones, not as the machine's figures.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A user limit is not a resistance figure</h3>
          <p>
            Marcy rates the MWM-990 for a 300 lb user and Total Gym rates the EVOLVE for 500 lb.
            Those figures are how heavy a person the frame is designed to carry, not how much you
            can lift on it. Stay inside them, and treat every one as the maker's figure, not ours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Ceiling Height, Footprint and Working Area</h2>
<p>
            Three measurements decide whether an all-in-one gym fits: the height of the frame, the
            floor it covers, and the space you need around it to train. The table gives the
            makers' figures for the five picks; where a source disagreed, the card says so.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machine</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Maker's height</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Maker's footprint (L x W)</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Body-Solid EXM2500S</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">83 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">85 x 61 in.; 89 x 79 in. with leg press</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Marcy MWM-990</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">78 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">68 x 42 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Body-Solid Best Fitness BFMG20</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">78 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">64 x 47 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">BowFlex Xtreme 2 SE</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">83.25 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">63 x 49 in.; 76 x 78 in. in use; 112 x 150 in. workout area</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Total Gym EVOLVE</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">45 in. unfolded</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">97 x 17 in.; folds to 50.5 x 17 x 8.5 in.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling.</strong> The stack and rod machines here stand 78 to a little over 83
            inches tall. On a lat pulldown you sit under the bar, so in use you need little more
            than the frame height, but two things eat into the margin. Rubber flooring or a platform
            raises the machine by its own thickness, and the uprights of a stack gym are usually
            assembled lying down and then stood up, which needs more height than the finished frame.
            Measure at the exact spot, under any beam, duct or soffit, and if the gap above the frame
            is only an inch or two, ask the maker how its uprights go up before you order. Basements
            and garages are where this goes wrong most often; our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for a garage</a>
            covers the same low-ceiling and cold-floor issues for cardio machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Footprint versus working area.</strong> The listed footprint is the frame. Press
            arms swing out, the leg developer needs room for your feet, and low-pulley rows and curls
            are done standing in front of the machine. BowFlex is unusually specific: it gives an
            in-use size of 76 by 78 inches for the Xtreme 2 SE and recommends a workout area of 112
            by 150 inches, more than three times the frame. That is a sensible rule for any
            all-in-one: allow several feet of clear floor in front of the low pulley and to the sides
            of the press arms. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill space requirements</a>
            uses the same method — footprint, plus a use zone, plus a safety margin — and it
            transfers directly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Your own height.</strong> BowFlex gives a suggested user height of 4 ft 6 in to
            6 ft 6 in for the Xtreme 2 SE. Marcy does not publish a height range for the MWM-990;
            BarBend notes that very tall or heavy users may find it harder to use, and an owner
            review we read said the same about range of motion at six feet. Seat adjustment, the
            reach of the press arms and the travel of the lat bar decide comfort more than the frame
            height. If you are well above or below average height, read the manual's adjustment
            ranges before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="assembly-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Delivery, Assembly and Getting It Into the Room</h2>
<p>
            All-in-one gyms arrive as heavy flat-packed boxes, and the stack versions are among the
            longest assembly jobs in home fitness. Makers rarely publish an assembly time, and we
            have not assembled these machines, so here is what the makers' delivery terms tell you,
            and how to plan.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Marcy MWM-990.</strong> Marcy says it ships by UPS in three separate boxes
            that may arrive on different days and are left where UPS normally leaves large packages,
            and that assembly is required. BarBend notes that tools are not included. Do not start
            until every box is there.</li>
            <li><strong>Body-Solid EXM2500S and BFMG20.</strong> Both ship as freight-sized
            packages; Iron Company lists the BFMG20's carton at about 64 by 24 by 8 inches. Body-Solid
            does not give a time. The stack plates are the heaviest part of the job and are best
            carried in a few at a time.</li>
            <li><strong>BowFlex Xtreme 2 SE.</strong> BowFlex offered parcel delivery for $199 or
            white-glove delivery for $399 on 19 October, the latter including delivery to the room of
            your choice, one flight of stairs, full assembly and packaging removal. For a first home
            gym that is often money well spent.</li>
            <li><strong>Total Gym EVOLVE.</strong> Total Gym says it arrives fully assembled. You
            unfold it, set the incline and train.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For any stack gym, plan for two people for most of a day, a clear floor larger than the
            finished machine to lay parts out, and the manual open at every step. The habits that
            prevent most problems are simple: assemble on the spot the machine will live, because
            moving a finished stack gym is hard and risky; leave bolts finger-tight until a whole
            sub-frame is square, then tighten in stages; route every cable exactly as drawn,
            checking that it sits in each pulley groove; and run the stack through its full travel
            with light weight before you load it. Recheck bolts after the first few sessions, as
            frames settle. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembling a treadmill</a>
            covers unboxing, tools and two-person lifting in more detail, and the same habits apply.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check the return terms before you open anything. BowFlex's store said on 19 October that
            unwanted equipment could be returned within 30 days of delivery for a refund of the
            product price, minus shipping and handling. Other sellers' terms vary, and a machine that
            has been assembled is far harder to send back, so read the current policy where you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="floor-loading" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Floor Loading, Flooring and Upstairs Rooms</h2>
<p>
            A home gym machine is heavy, but its weight is spread over a frame several feet long, so
            on a ground-floor concrete slab it is rarely a structural concern. Upstairs, on a timber
            floor, it is worth doing the arithmetic.
          </p>
          <p>
            Take the heaviest machine here as an example. Body-Solid lists the EXM2500S at 578 lb on
            its product page, and its footprint at 85 by 61 inches, which is about 36 square feet.
            Add a 250 lb user and the total is a little over 800 lb, or roughly 23 lb per square
            foot averaged over the frame. For comparison, the International Residential Code's
            design live load for living areas is 40 lb per square foot, and 30 for sleeping rooms.
            On those numbers an average spread looks comfortable, but averages hide two things. The
            load is not spread evenly: it sits on the feet of the frame and is concentrated under the
            stack. And floors are designed with many other assumptions, such as the span and spacing
            of the joists, that this arithmetic does not see.
          </p>
          <p>
            Practical rules, as general guidance rather than engineering advice:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Prefer the ground floor or a slab</strong> for a stack gym, especially the
            heavier commercial-style ones.</li>
            <li><strong>Upstairs, place the machine near a load-bearing wall</strong> and across the
            joists rather than in the middle of a span, and do not combine it with a loaded rack and
            plate storage in the same corner.</li>
            <li><strong>Choose a lighter design if the floor is doubtful.</strong> BowFlex lists the
            Xtreme 2 SE at 174 lb and Total Gym's EVOLVE frame at about 100 lb; both are far easier
            on a floor than any stack gym.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask a structural engineer</strong> for an older house, a long joist span, a
            floor that already bounces, or anything unusual.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Whatever the floor, put the machine on rubber flooring. It protects the floor from the
            frame's feet, stops a machine creeping on a smooth surface, and cuts the clank of a stack
            being set down. Horse-stall mats or interlocking rubber tiles under the whole frame and
            working area are usual; our
            <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a>
            compares the options, and the guide to
            <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifting platforms</a>
            covers a timber platform if you also lift free weights next to the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Reading a Home Gym Listing, Including Marketplace Machines</h2>
<p>
            Search Amazon for a home gym and the results are dominated by stack gyms from brands that
            exist mainly on marketplaces, often at prices well below the machines on this page. Some
            may be perfectly serviceable. The problem is verification: for the ones we looked at, we
            could not find a maker's specification published anywhere except the listings, and
            listings for what looks like the same frame gave different stack weights. Six checks
            separate a usable listing from a guess.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Is the resistance a stack, and how heavy?</strong> Look for the stack in
            pounds and the size of each plate. "Up to 400 lb resistance" with no stack figure usually
            means a rated cable or a ratio, not iron.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the user limit stated, and where?</strong> A figure only in the title, or
            different figures in the title and the specification table, is a warning sign.</li>
            <li><strong>Are assembled dimensions given in all three directions?</strong> Many
            listings give the box size or omit the height. Without the height you cannot check your
            ceiling.</li>
            <li><strong>What is the cable rated to?</strong> Body-Solid states 2,200 lb-test aircraft
            cable for both of its machines here. A listing that does not mention the cable at all
            gives you nothing to judge.</li>
            <li><strong>Who stands behind the warranty?</strong> A long warranty from a seller with
            no address, manual or parts page is worth less than a short one from a maker that sells
            replacement cables. Before you buy, check that you can find a manual and a parts list.</li>
            <li><strong>Can you buy replacement cables?</strong> Cables and pulleys wear. Marcy lists
            a replacement cable set for its MWM stack gyms on its own store; with an unbranded
            machine you may be left matching lengths yourself.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If a marketplace machine passes all six, it may be a reasonable budget buy. If it fails
            two or three, the Marcy or the Best Fitness gym costs a little more and comes from a
            maker you can call.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-and-recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Buying Used: The Old Recalls to Check</h2>
<p>
            All-in-one gyms last a long time and resell constantly, so the used market is full of
            older machines, including some that were recalled. These are the home gym recalls we
            found in the CPSC database on 19 October 2026 for the brands on this page. None covers a
            machine carded above.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Recalled machine</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Problem described</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Status in the notice</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex Power Pro XL, XTL and XTLU with Lat Tower (January 2004)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Backboard bench could collapse in the incline position; Lat Tower could rotate forward and fall</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remedy no longer available; discard, do not resell</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex Power Pro and Ultimate built before September 2002 (November 2004)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Seat pin could break or disengage; incline bracket could break</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remedy no longer available; discard, do not resell</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex Ultimate 2 (2006, 2007 and 2009 notices)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Leg extension cable could release; rod box fasteners could loosen; unlatched seat rail could fall</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair kits for later notices; the 2006 notice now says dispose of the product</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Total Gym 1000 and Total Gym Pro, sold 1997–2001 (May 2002)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Handles could detach and cables break</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair kit offered at the time</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Total Gym XL, infomercial sales January–February 2002 (May 2002)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lever pin supporting the raised rails could fail</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free safety bracket offered at the time</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The two Power Pro notices are the ones to take most seriously: they say the original
            firm is no longer in business, the remedy is no longer available, and owners should
            discard the machines and not donate or resell them. If a listing for a used BowFlex does
            not show the model name on the frame, ask for a photo before you go to see it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond recalls, a used stack or rod gym is mostly a cable check. Run every cable through
            its full travel and look for fraying, kinks, flattened spots and cracked pulleys; budget
            for a new set of cables if they look tired, and walk away if the seller cannot tell you
            the model. Rods lose some resistance with age by BowFlex's own account, so a used rod gym
            may feel lighter than its ratings. For the wider question of what to buy new and what
            secondhand, our guide to
            <a href="/commercial-gym-equipment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial gym equipment</a>
            explains how used commercial machines differ from home ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want to squat, deadlift and press heavy</strong>, no all-in-one gym on
            this page replaces a barbell. A rack, bar and plates cost a similar amount to a good
            stack gym and grow much further. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat racks for a home gym</a>
            is the place to start, and a
            <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight bench</a>
            completes the setup.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If cables are the main attraction</strong>, a functional trainer gives longer
            cable travel and more angles than the fixed stations of an all-in-one. Our
            <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cable machine guide</a>
            compares them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If space or budget is tight</strong>, a pair of adjustable dumbbells and a bench
            covers most of what an all-in-one gym does in a corner of a room. Our
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbell guide</a>
            covers the options and the recalls. For pull-ups and dips without a full machine, see
            our guide to
            <a href="/best-power-towers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">power towers</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are equipping a shared or commercial space</strong>, residential machines
            are not designed for it; Marcy, for one, says the MWM-990 is not intended for commercial
            use. Our
            <a href="/commercial-gym-equipment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial gym equipment guide</a>
            covers what changes at that level.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If cardio is the priority</strong>, an all-in-one gym will not replace it. Many
            home gyms pair one strength station with one cardio machine; our
            <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to cardio machines for home</a>
            helps with that half of the room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best home gym machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people who can afford it, a commercial-style weight-stack gym such as the Body-Solid EXM2500S, because a stack feels like gym machines and lasts decades. On a budget, the Marcy MWM-990 gives a real 150 lb stack. If the machine must go upstairs or be light, the BowFlex Xtreme 2 SE is the rod alternative, and for low ceilings an incline trainer like the Total Gym EVOLVE fits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a weight stack better than BowFlex rods?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better for everyone. A weight stack gives the same resistance through the whole rep and feels like a gym machine, but it is heavy and needs add-on plates to grow. Rods are light, cheaper for the headline figure and easy on joints at the start of a rep, but they resist least at the start and BowFlex says their ratings are estimates that degrade with use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much ceiling height do I need for a home gym machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The stack and rod machines on this page stand about 78 to 83 inches tall, so a standard 8-foot ceiling usually works. Add the thickness of any flooring, check for beams and ducts at the exact spot, and allow extra height for assembly, because uprights are often stood up from lying flat. Incline trainers like the Total Gym EVOLVE need far less.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a home gym machine go upstairs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, but choose the design with the floor in mind. A rod gym such as the 174 lb BowFlex Xtreme 2 SE or an incline trainer is far lighter than a stack gym. For a stack gym upstairs, place it near a load-bearing wall and across the joists, use rubber flooring, and ask a structural engineer about older houses or long spans. This is general guidance, not engineering advice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to assemble a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Makers rarely publish a time, and we have not assembled these machines. A weight-stack gym is one of the longest assembly jobs in home fitness, so plan for two people and most of a day. BowFlex offers white-glove delivery with full assembly for the Xtreme 2 SE, and Total Gym says the EVOLVE arrives fully assembled.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 150 lb weight stack enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For many beginners and intermediate lifters, yes, for chest presses, rows, curls and extensions. Stronger lifters often outgrow 150 lb on lat pulldowns and leg work. Remember that the felt resistance at each station depends on how the cable and levers are arranged, so it may not match the number on the pin.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Home Gym Machine</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a large, heavy purchase into one that disappoints, does not fit, or is hard to send back.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing headline resistance across designs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 150 lb stack, 210 lb of rods and a 500 lb user limit are not comparable numbers. Rods resist least at the start of each rep and are rated as a total for both sides; stack stations vary with their cable and lever layout; bodyweight trainers depend on the incline. Compare machines of the same type, and judge progress against your own sessions.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the frame but not the working area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The listed footprint is only the frame. Press arms swing out, rows are done in front of the low pulley, and BowFlex recommends a workout area more than three times the Xtreme 2 SE's frame. Mark the frame and a clear zone on the floor with tape before you order, and check the ceiling height at the same spot.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Opening every box before the last one arrives</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Stack gyms often ship in several boxes on different days. Starting assembly with a box missing leaves a half-built frame in the middle of the room and makes a return far harder. Check every box against the parts list when it arrives, and keep the packaging until the machine is assembled and working.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used machine without the model name</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Several older BowFlex home gyms were recalled, and two notices say the remedy is no longer available and the machines should be discarded, not resold. Ask for a photo of the model name on the frame, check it against the CPSC database, and inspect every cable and pulley before paying.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

